{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues that decentralized networks are more resilient specifically because they use lazy/eventual consistency while large services use strict consistency. This conflates an architectural choice (consistency model) with the degree of centralization, so the argument fails the problem's comparison as stated. The problem asks about a 'decentralized network of small, independently operated services' versus 'one large service with strictly more total internal redundancy' — it does not stipulate their consistency protocols. A large service can be built on an eventual-consistency or partition-tolerant design (e.g., a globally replicated AP datastore with massive internal redundancy), and a decentralized network can require synchronous consensus (SRC_001 even concedes this in a countercondition). Under such a configuration, SRC_001's claimed mechanism vanishes: the decentralized network halts on a failure while the large service partitions and continues. Because the causal argument rests on an incidental feature that is not implied by either side of the comparison, it cannot establish that decentralization itself yields greater resilience to correlated failure.","counterexample":[{"large_service":"A single globally-replicated AP key-value store (eventual consistency, partitions under failure) with N internal replicas","decentralized_network":"N independent small services using PBFT/multi-site synchronous consensus that halts when one member fails"}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 asserts that the DAG-vs-cycle structure of the dependency graph distinguishes truly independent failures from merely uncorrelated ones. But the claim conflates two different structures: the text first says independence requires a DAG with distinct roots, then says conditional dependence arises from 'cycles in the transitive closure.' This is a genuine internal inconsistency, because cycles in the transitive closure of a dependency graph are not the same as sharing a common ancestor; a DAG with a single shared root has no cycles in its transitive closure, yet by the argument's own shared-root countercondition it still produces conditionally dependent failures. The proposed distinguishing evidence (mapping the graph to check for cycles) therefore does not actually identify the hidden correlation the problem asks about — it would miss the common-root case, and it would over-flag cyclic dependencies that may still be independently operated. Because the evidence criterion does not match the claimed mechanism, the target fails to answer the problem's request for evidence that distinguishes truly independent from merely-uncorrelated-until-shared-cause failures.","counterexample":[{"graph":"DAG with a single shared root A feeding both B and C; no cycles in the transitive closure","observation":"By the cycle-check evidence, failures of B and C would be classified as independent (no cycle), yet the shared root A is exactly the hidden common cause the countercondition identifies."}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}